 Axios Markets By
Emily Peck and Matt Phillips
 &middot; 
Oct 16, 2026
 
 
 😎 Friday! S&amp;P 500 futures are slightly higher, and Nasdaq 100 futures are flat this morning amid lingering weakness in AI-related stocks.

 The slight boost may be due to investors pricing in higher odds that the Fed stays on hold at its meeting later this month after chairman Kevin Warsh's comments overnight . Today, after years of trying to ignore the corporate bond market, Emily explains why it matters — especially right now. Matt digs into the current market dilemma that is CoreWeave. 

 In 1,199 words, a 4.5-minute read. 


 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 
1 big thing: Bond investors sound the alarms
 
 By Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The adults have entered the AI chat — bond investors this week looked up from their massive buying binge and asked themselves, "Hey, are we sure these investments are going to pay out?"

 Why it matters: The AI boom is looking a little wobbly these days, at least when it comes to all the borrowed money that's fueling it. 

 " Credit markets are where some of the AI boom's weaknesses can show up first," Robert Schiffman, a senior credit analyst at Bloomberg Intelligence, tells Axios. If you look back at the history of many big investing booms that later went bust —  from railroads to housing — the first warning signs were with the bonds. The big picture: Bond investors — the adults in the room — can often spot trouble brewing in a market.

 Why's that? Stock investors place a much higher value on growth. Their basic question is: How high will this go? But bond investors are all about risk. They just want to know: Will I get paid back? Follow the money: A few factors have converged to cause bond investors to rethink AI debt. 

 At the macro level, the Iran war that began earlier this year led to a surge in energy prices that drove up inflation. And that has helped drive interest rates higher, creating a potential problem for weaker borrowers that need to raise fresh money or refinance existing loans. Zoom in: That's especially true for the so-called neoclouds, "the newest layer of the AI economy," as Mark Malek, chief investment officer at Muriel Siebert, calls them. 

 These are companies racing, alongside Big Tech, to build and rent out AI compute. You've likely not heard of them — Crusoe, Nebius, Nscale, Lambda and, most prominently, CoreWeave. Unlike the hyperscalers — Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon — the neoclouds are more dependent on borrowing to build data centers. "This is the subprime tranche of the AI trade," Gil Luria, head of technology research at D.A. Davidson, tells Axios. Catch up quick: Investors have been selling off CoreWeave's bonds for weeks, worried that the revenues from AI may not be enough to sustain its growing debt burden. 

 The prices for its bonds have now tumbled, pushing its yields (or interest rates on the bonds) into distressed territory. The accelerated deterioration shook the broader AI trade. Bonds issued to fund several different data centers have also fallen in recent weeks, as the Financial Times noted — one investor called it a "Darwin moment," where only the strongest would survive. Between the lines: Luria, perhaps inadvertently, helped push the sell-off this week. He went on the "Prof. G Markets" podcast Wednesday, and warned that CoreWeave's stock price could go to zero if the AI investment cycle turns.

 Attention on the company started building the previous day when venture capitalist Chamath Palihapitiya posted a chart showing CoreWeave's record-high bond yield. To cap it off, Elon Musk replied, "Yikes." "All this stuff was coming together," Luria says. As for his quotes? "I don't know that they were the reason or the problem, but it probably didn't help." Reality check: Investors and analysts still believe AI demand is strong enough to keep the cycle turning.

 But the recent weakness in CoreWeave and others shows where a break in the excitement is likely to start. It's unclear if there would be contagion from there. 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 
Bonus chart: Swaps meet reality
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Axios/Matt Phillips Five-year credit default swaps — a kind of insurance that investors buy to protect them against a company defaulting on its debt — on CoreWeave yesterday jumped to nearly 1,100 basis points (or 11 percentage points) for every $100,000 of debt to be insured.

 At that price, it would cost about $1.1 million a year to insure $10 million of CoreWeave bonds against default. The costs of such insurance did reverse later in the day as the company's share price bounced. 
 
 
